# Extending SMF RESTful API — Overview & Hooks

The API is designed so that third-party mods can add their own resources and influence request handling through SMF's standard integration hooks — without modifying this mod's files.

## Available hooks

{header-rows=0}
|= *`integrate_api_routes`* (`Router $router`) | < |
| Register your own endpoints | Fires when the router is initialized |
|= *`integrate_api_pre_dispatch`* (`Request $request`) | < |
| Global logic (throttling, CORS preflight); may throw `ApiException` | Fires after the request is parsed, before authentication |
|= *`integrate_api_authenticate`* (`Request $request`, `?int &$idMember`) | < |
| Your own schemes (OAuth/JWT): set `$idMember` | Fires at the start of authentication |
|= *`integrate_api_pre_send`* (`array &$payload`, `int &$status`, `array &$headers`) | < |
| Adjust the body, status, and headers (CORS, rate limiting, wrappers) | Fires before the response is sent |

## Where hooks come from

All four hooks are standard SMF `integrate_*` hooks registered via `add_integration_function()`. Your mod's `Integration::hooks()` registers them the same way SMF itself does — see [Adding a Resource](/guide/extending/adding-resource).

## Version prefix and route style

Routes are registered *without* a version prefix. The leading version segment (`v1`) is stripped and validated by the `Router` itself, so patterns stay version-agnostic. The client addresses the resource as `index.php?action=api&path=v1/widgets`. See [Adding a Resource](/guide/extending/adding-resource) for the nested collection rule and reserved parameter names.
